{"kind":"request","text":"Getting started never says to create a workroom. docs/getting-started.md clones, builds, and hands off to the documentation map, but it never runs `gs init`. A reader who follows it exactly ends with bin/gs and bin/gitseq-mcp and no workroom, and the next thing they try fails. Every other page that needs a workroom opens with `gs init --repo \"$REPO\" --operator alice` — publish-and-audit, configure-an-agent, run-a-work-loop, deploy-a-resident, end-to-end, and eleven reference pages — so getting-started is the single page missing the step, not a convention the set has chosen. The docset gates did not catch it because the page's only fenced block is `text`, not `sh`, so the example runner never executes it and the surface gate only checks that a page exists per subcommand, not that the onboarding path is complete. This is the same step the solo-adoption route in request b7087c81 turns on: run `gs init` inside a repository you already work in. Reported by hugh 2026-08-10.","body":{"conditions":"docs/getting-started.md carries the `gs init` step in a runnable block, with the operator argument and what the command creates, so a reader who follows the page top to bottom ends with a working workroom rather than only built binaries; the page states plainly that init is safe to run inside an existing repository and touches no branch, HEAD, index, or working tree; the block is `sh` so the docset example gate executes it; the page's rests_on cites the artifact for the init behaviour it describes, not only the request; `make test` passes including the docset gates; the change is reviewed by codex at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}